.TH MBT 1 "2026-11-12" "Mojt Linux" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mbt \- prepare private build tools and create Mojt build/package results
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mbt env
[--profile image|kernel] --source-manifest FILE --output DIRECTORY [--dry-run] [-y]
.PP
.B mbt build kernel
--plan FILE --config FILE --config-sha256 SHA256 --environment DIRECTORY
--output DIRECTORY [--source DIRECTORY] [--resume] [--jobs N] [--dry-run] [-y]
.PP
.B mbt package
{kernel|base|system|pack|verify} ...
.PP
.B mbt bootstrap
--base-package FILE --base-artifact ID --base-context FILE
--kernel-registry DIRECTORY --kernel-artifact ID --system-payload DIRECTORY
--system-generation NAME --output DIRECTORY [--system-version VERSION]
[--system-revision N] [--with-build-tools] [--with-installer-inputs] [--dry-run]
[--hardware-profile thinkpad-x1-carbon-3 --firmware-root DIRECTORY]
.PP
.B mbt bootstrap
--inputs DIRECTORY --target-root DIRECTORY --efi-directory DIRECTORY
[--expected-root-uuid UUID --expected-efi-partuuid UUID] [--dry-run]
[--system-generation NAME]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Experimental interface for review. MBT delegates to the existing environment,
Kernel build, native artifact and image tools. Use each command's --help for flags.
Building and packaging do not install or activate their result. Bootstrap uses
MPM installation/deployment libraries on a new image or empty mounted target. Existing-system updates
belong to MPM. No command implicitly reboots or changes a global compiler.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B env
Create a new private environment with pinned binary tools. The initial provider
is Nix; the tool inventory and library closure are retained beside the environment.
--dry-run previews without writing the environment or GC roots. No registry of
names or separate plan command is needed. Keep a created environment in place.
Acquisition follows provider-store permissions. Mojt's root-owned store currently
needs an administrator to import tools; make that pinned environment readable by
the ordinary builder. MBT does not grant store writes or start a provider daemon.
.TP
.B build kernel
Compile a separate versioned Kernel and matching modules as an ordinary user.
Source, configuration hash, policy and private environment remain explicit.
--dry-run validates inputs without fetching sources, compiling or writing output.
--resume requires the same verified inputs and an eligible incomplete workspace;
it does not repair arbitrary failed output. No automatic privilege escalation.
.TP
.B package
Create and independently verify a native archive from a complete build or
retained release. Packaging revision and original build provenance are distinct.
verify checks an existing archive without installing it. Packaging has no
--dry-run option in this initial interface; it writes a new explicit output.
.TP
.B bootstrap
With --output, create a NEW regular UEFI/GPT native-v2 image with serial console and VirtIO.
Requires verified exact Base/Kernel inputs, explicit Base compatibility bounds
and an account-enabled step-10 System payload. --dry-run validates inputs without
creating an image or registry. Actual creation requires root and an interactive
terminal to set the new guest password. No automatic sudo or password flags.
.PP
The optional thinkpad-x1-carbon-3 profile selects local password login, USB/SATA
storage and keyboard support, and graphics/network drivers. --firmware-root names
an extracted Debian firmware root with Intel 7265 firmware, regulatory database
and upstream licenses. Firmware/licenses become inventoried System content;
complete modules remain in the exact native Kernel artifact. Both mounts are
read-only. Existing-system updates preserve the bound hardware/console policy.
Normal boot does not scan firmware content. Independent firmware artifacts and
general firmware/service upgrade transitions are not implemented.
.PP
Installs Base/System and stages/selects the initial exact deployment through
MPM libraries in a private directory before image formatting or mounted-target copying. Fixed manager code runs using
the accepted System Python/Nix in private mount/PID namespaces. No archive hooks,
host Nix database mutation or loop mapping is used. Result/selection and boot-file
hashes must agree before publication; no direct-boot bypass is exposed.
An existing image/installation cannot be adopted or a failed preparation resumed.
This assembles prebuilt inputs; it does not rebuild the entire distribution.
Preflight checks prepared manager code, host tool locations and static BusyBox
before password entry. Root also probes private PID/mount namespace and chroot
entry; ordinary-user preview explicitly reports this privileged probe as not run.
This does not establish complete construction or guest boot acceptance.
.PP
--with-installer-inputs (requires --with-build-tools) carries the frozen local
Base/context, Kernel registry, System export, static BusyBox and optional
firmware in persistent /var/lib/mojt/installer-inputs. --inputs DIRECTORY
verifies that closed versioned data and supplies the existing exact input flags.
Competing artifact/provider flags cannot be mixed with it. It loads no hooks/code
and carries no source installation passwords. SHA256 is not authentication.
.PP
--target-root and --efi-directory select separate, already mounted empty ext4
and FAT32 partitions on the same GPT disk. Root may have an empty lost+found;
existing EFI trees, alias/child mounts, another mounted partition or active swap
on the disk and unsupported storage profiles are refused. Even preview requires
root to inspect block metadata. Actual writes require both expected identity
flags from preview. No partitioning, formatting, resizing or NVRAM entry is done.
Use an explicit private image-tool environment to supply the disk/EFI tools.
.PP
Construction is shared with the image path and binds the actual target UUID.
Verified root content is copied/flushed before the fallback EFI directory is
published. Failure retains partial data and an incomplete marker; there is no
adoption/resume or automatic cleanup. A late flush failure reports that EFI was
already published and the target may be bootable. Mounted installation ownership
stays with root. The initial construction receipt is mounted-install.json under
the new installation's /var/lib/mojt; MPM records remain current boot authority.
.SH DELIVERY
Builder-enabled System images include the Python frontend, existing helper code,
this manual, fixed assembly assets and a pinned Kernel recipe example. The
installed builder can assemble its successor without a checkout. Compilers are acquired separately
in private environments. Ordinary System compositions need not carry MBT.
Public operations first copy the fixed code/assets to a private execution
directory with explicit permissions, then start isolated System Python there.
The source checkout and home permissions are unchanged. Temporary execution
files are removed after completion; bootstrap retains their content identity
and inventory as builder-runtime.json. SHA256 evidence is not authentication.
The launcher preserves an explicitly selected private tool PATH; bootstrap
refuses tools selected from the shared /var/lib/mojt/ports profile.
.SH LIMITS
Writing an image to a physical disk remains a separate explicit operation. The low-level
image helper retains its legacy default; use mbt bootstrap for native-v2.
There are no arbitrary recipes/hooks, saved-plan executor,
named-environment registry, GC or source rebuild of the entire distribution.
Runtime closure checks are not a universal libc/plugin compatibility guarantee.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mpm (1)
